---
import ToolLayout from "../../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { MassConverter } from "../../../components/converters/MassConverter.js";
import { getToolByPath } from "../../../content/tools.js";

const tool = getToolByPath("/tools/converters/mass")!;
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  purpose="Convert kilograms to pounds. The converter opens on 1 kilogram, which is 2.204623 pounds. Ounces, grams, stone, and tonnes are separate units."
>
  <MassConverter client:load />

  <Fragment slot="details">
    <h2>Convert kilograms to pounds</h2>
    <p>
      Leave the units on kilograms and pounds, or choose them from the two menus. One kilogram converts to 2.204623
      pounds. The exact factor is 1 lb = 0.45359237 kg, and 1 ÷ 0.45359237 is 2.2046226218… before the six-decimal display.
      Choosing ounces instead gives ounces on their own: 1 kilogram is 35.273962 ounces. The result is never split into pounds and ounces together.
    </p>

    <h2>Common mass conversions</h2>
    <table>
      <caption>Mass factors used by this converter</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">From</th>
          <th scope="col">Equals</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1 pound</td>
          <td>0.45359237 kilograms</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>1 kilogram</td>
          <td>2.204623 pounds</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>1 ounce</td>
          <td>28.349523125 grams</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>1 stone</td>
          <td>6.35029318 kilograms (14 pounds)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>1 metric tonne</td>
          <td>1,000 kilograms</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      For length, use the <a href="/tools/converters/length/">length converter</a>. For temperature, use the
      <a href="/tools/converters/temperature/">Celsius to Fahrenheit converter</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why "mass / weight"?</h2>
    <p>
      Technically, mass (kilograms, pounds) and weight (a force) are different physical quantities, but in everyday
      language "weight" almost always means mass — how much something weighs on a kitchen or bathroom scale. This
      tool converts mass units, labelled as "mass / weight" to match how people actually search for it.
    </p>

    <h2>Accepted inputs</h2>
    <p>
      One number, then any two of milligram, gram, kilogram, metric tonne, ounce, pound, and stone. The converter opens on 1 kilogram to pounds.
    </p>

    <h2>How to read the result</h2>
    <p>
      The figure is shown to six decimal places. The kilogram-to-pound and kilogram-to-ounce examples above use that display. 1 stone = 6.35029318 kilograms, which is 14 pounds.
    </p>

    <h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Conversion factors use standard international definitions (e.g. 1 pound = 0.45359237 kilograms exactly).</li>
      <li>This tool does not account for local variations in gravity, which would matter only for true weight-as-force calculations, not everyday mass conversions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reference</h2>
    <p>
      The pound-to-kilogram conversion (1 lb = 0.45359237 kg) is the internationally agreed exact value from the
      1959 International Yard and Pound Agreement, as documented in NIST Handbook 44.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
